    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">02</span><div class="num">Chapter Two</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="fast">Why it's the fastest money — and the easiest to do badly</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Affiliate marketing is fast because there's nothing to build — you can recommend a tool today and earn tomorrow. It's easy to do badly because that same speed tempts you to recommend for the payout, which quietly spends the trust the whole model runs on.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Every affiliate recommendation is a tiny withdrawal or deposit on your credibility. Recommend something great, and the reader trusts you more next time. Recommend something for the commission, and even if they buy once, they stop believing you — and belief is the only thing you were ever selling. <strong>You can't out-volume a trust problem.</strong></p>

    <div class="photo-quote reveal">
      <div class="photo dark" style="position:absolute;inset:0;background-image:url(/images/library/portrait-woman-golden-hour.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
      <div class="veil"></div>
      <div class="inner"><p>Recommend only what you'd recommend for free. The commission is the bonus, never the reason.</p></div>
    </div>

    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Chasing the highest-commission program instead of the most honest recommendation. A 50% payout on a tool you don't believe in is worth nothing — because you'll only sell it once, to people who won't trust you again.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">03</span><div class="num">Chapter Three</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="links">How affiliate links actually work</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">An affiliate link is a normal link with a tracking code attached to it. When someone clicks it and buys, the code tells the company you sent them, and you're paid a set percentage or flat fee — at no extra cost to the buyer.</p>
    <p class="reveal">You get the link by joining a program — free to join, in almost every case. The company handles the product, the checkout, and the payment; your only job is the honest recommendation and the link. Most programs use a cookie window, so you're often credited even if the person buys a few days after clicking.</p>

    <div class="framework reveal">
      <span class="tag"><span>✦</span> The Affiliate Loop</span>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="node"><div class="h">You Recommend</div><div class="t">An honest pick, in content someone's already reading.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">They Click + Buy</div><div class="t">Your tracked link credits the sale to you.</div></div>
        <div class="arrow">→</div>
        <div class="node"><div class="h">You're Paid</div><div class="t">A percentage or flat fee — the buyer pays nothing extra.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>Disclosure isn't optional and isn't a weakness. A simple line — "some links here are affiliate links, which means I may earn a small commission at no cost to you" — is legally required in most places and actually <em>builds</em> trust. Honest people disclose. Readers know it.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter left reveal"><span class="bignum">04</span><div class="num">Chapter Four</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="trust">Choosing what to recommend</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Recommend only tools you actually use and would stand behind for free. The best affiliate pick sits where "I genuinely use this," "my reader needs this," and "this pays a fair commission" overlap — in that order of priority.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Notice the order. Use and need come first; commission comes last. A tool you love that pays modestly will always out-earn a tool you don't, because you can recommend it with a straight face a hundred times. <strong>The commission rate is a tiebreaker, never the deciding vote.</strong></p>

    <div class="marginal reveal">
      <p>A clean filter before you add any affiliate link: <em>would I still tell someone to buy this if there were no commission at all?</em> If the answer is no, the link doesn't go in.</p>
      <span class="note">your taste is the product ✶</span>
    </div>

    <div class="box mistake reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✕</span> Common Mistake</span><p>Leading with a program because it's famous, not because it fits. Amazon Associates is the obvious example — everyone joins it, and for most creators in this niche it pays so little per sale that leading with it is a poor use of your best asset. It's worth understanding, not worth building on. Teach it; don't lean your income on it.</p></div>

    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">05</span><div class="num">Chapter Five</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="clicks">Where the clicks come from without an audience</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Affiliate clicks come from content people are already searching for — a helpful blog post or pin answering a real question, with your recommendation inside it. You don't need a following; you need to be found at the moment someone's deciding what to buy.</p>
    <p class="reveal">This is the faceless advantage again. Someone types "best tool for X" into Pinterest or Google, lands on your genuinely useful comparison, and clicks your link because you answered their question well. <strong>The audience isn't the requirement — being the answer is.</strong></p>

